  "result": "The premise is the problem: planting further behind the ball doesn't give you more power. You shouldn't plant behind it at all.\n\nPlant your foot **beside** the ball, with your toes pointing at your target. Coaching guides suggest roughly 6–8 inches to the side. Level with the ball, or a touch ahead of it, keeps the shot low.\n\nHere is why behind feels better but isn't. When your plant foot is behind the ball, you can't get your knee and chest over it. You lean back, and your kicking foot catches the underside of the ball. The ball climbs and can go over the bar. This is probably the cause of your occasional misses. The longer swing does add some speed, but the lift costs you more than that, and a ball over the bar scores nothing.\n\nWhere the power actually comes from:\n- a solid plant beside the ball\n- locking your ankle at contact\n- following all the way through\n- a run-up that's smooth rather than rushed\n\nYour goal is the corner with power, and a low, hard, accurate shot is the one that does that.\n\nTry this in your next practice session. Take five penalties with your plant foot beside the ball, and five with it clearly behind. Watch how high each one goes.\n\nQuick check: if your shots are sailing high, which way should you move your plant foot, and why?\n\nIf you want, I can build a short lesson on plant-foot position and shot height.\n\nSources:\n- [Supplemental Instep Drive Instruction](https://coachingamericansoccer.com/advanced-skills/supplemental-soccer-instep-drive-instruction/)\n- [Adding Power to the Instep Drive](https://coachingamericansoccer.com/?p=9457)\n- [Shooting in Soccer](https://expertfootball.com/?p=shooting)",
